    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For a drill that hangs holes in plasterboard twice a year, the standard version is genuinely fine and the money is better spent elsewhere. For anything that cuts, the gap is not subtle.
        </p>

        <div id="what-hp-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What HP Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            HP stands for High Performance, and Ryobi uses it across two different product types, which is where most of the confusion comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a tool, HP means the motor is brushless. Instead of carbon brushes physically contacting a spinning commutator to switch current through the motor windings, an electronic controller does the switching. That removes a wearing part, removes a source of friction and heat, and lets the controller adapt power delivery to the load.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a battery, HP means high-output cells and supporting electronics capable of delivering substantially more continuous current than a standard pack. The voltage is unchanged — every ONE+ battery is 18 volts — and what differs is how hard the pack can push.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important consequence is that the two halves work together. A brushless HP tool can accept far more current than a brushed one, but only if the battery can supply it. Put a standard 2.0 amp-hour pack on an HP circular saw and the saw is limited by the battery, not the motor, and you have paid for performance you cannot access.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not a con; it is just poorly communicated at the point of sale. Both halves are still fully compatible with everything else in the platform, as our guide on ONE+ battery interchangeability covers, so nothing is wasted. But the marketing claims about HP performance assume the matched pair.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE THING TO UNDERSTAND</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">HP is a brushless tool plus a high-output battery. You need both.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An HP tool on a standard pack is limited by the battery. An HP battery on a brushed tool works fine and runs cooler but cannot make a brushed motor into a brushless one. The performance figures quoted for HP assume the matched pair, and with one half you get roughly half the benefit.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="brushless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Motor Difference, and What It Buys
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brushless is not marketing language; it describes a genuinely different way of commutating a motor, and the advantages are measurable.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a brushed motor, spring-loaded carbon blocks press against a rotating commutator to switch current through the windings as the armature turns. That contact is simple, cheap and reliable, and it also produces friction, heat, electrical noise and dust, and the brushes themselves wear away and eventually need replacing or end the motor's life.
          </p>

          <p>
            A brushless motor replaces that mechanical switching with electronics. Sensors or back-EMF detection tell a controller where the rotor is, and the controller energises the windings in sequence. Nothing rubs, so there is less friction, less heat and no wearing contact.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Brushed (Standard)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Brushless (HP)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Efficiency</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower, friction and heat losses</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Runtime per charge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Baseline</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Typically 20-50% longer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power for size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heat under load</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">More</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Less</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Motor lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited by brush wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Much longer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Behaviour under load</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bogs down</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Controller maintains output</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repairability</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushes replaceable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Controller failure ends it</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The row that matters most in use is the second to last. A brushed motor meeting resistance simply slows down. A brushless controller senses the load and pushes more current to hold speed, which is why a brushless saw feels like it is cutting rather than struggling. In a long rip cut or a hard driving job, that difference is obvious within seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            The efficiency gain also compounds with the battery. Less energy wasted as heat means more of the pack's capacity reaches the workpiece, which is where the runtime improvement comes from. It is not that HP tools have bigger batteries; they waste less of what they are given. Our general guide on brushless versus brushed drills covers the technology across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hp-batteries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Battery Half
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pack side is less discussed and just as important, particularly for the demanding tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every lithium cell has a maximum safe continuous discharge rate. Ask for more and the cell heats, the voltage sags, and the pack's protection circuit eventually cuts out to prevent damage. Standard ONE+ packs use cells sized for ordinary drilling and driving duty, which is entirely appropriate for what most people do.
          </p>

          <p>
            HP packs use cells with higher discharge capability and electronics built around that. The result is that a demanding tool can draw what it needs without the pack sagging, which shows up as maintained speed under load, less heat in your hand, and no unexpected shutdowns partway through a cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity and current capability are correlated but separate. A larger pack has more cells sharing the load, so each works less hard, which is why a 6.0 amp-hour standard pack outperforms a 2.0 amp-hour standard pack in a saw even though neither is an HP battery. The HP designation is about cell quality on top of that.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical hierarchy for demanding tools, best to worst: HP high-capacity pack, HP standard-capacity pack, large standard pack, small standard pack. A 6.0 amp-hour standard battery will run a brushless saw acceptably; a 1.5 amp-hour standard battery will make it feel broken.
          </p>

          <p>
            An HP pack in a brushed tool is entirely fine and slightly beneficial, since the tool draws what it needs and the pack barely notices, so it runs cool and holds voltage well. There is no reason to avoid it beyond cost and weight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Where the Difference Is Real
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four categories where HP genuinely changes what the tool can do rather than improving it marginally.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Anything That Cuts Wood</h3>

          <p>
            Circular saws, reciprocating saws, jigsaws and chainsaws are the clearest case. Cutting is a sustained high-current demand, exactly the condition where a brushed motor bogs and a standard pack sags. The difference between a brushed ONE+ circular saw and an HP one in a long cut through framing timber is the difference between a tool you tolerate and one you reach for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoor Power Equipment</h3>

          <p>
            Mowers, blowers, string trimmers and pole saws run continuously under load for long periods, which is the worst case for efficiency losses. HP versions run noticeably longer per charge and keep their speed when the grass gets thick. If you are considering cordless outdoor equipment on ONE+, HP is close to mandatory rather than optional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Impact Wrenches</h3>

          <p>
            Loosening a seized bolt is a short burst of extremely high demand, and the brushless controller's ability to deliver full torque without stalling is exactly what you want. A brushed impact wrench simply cannot reach the same breakaway torque.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Any Tool You Use Daily</h3>

          <p>
            If you are a tradesperson or you genuinely use a tool several times a week, the longer runtime and the absence of brush wear change the ownership experience over years, not just the feel in a single job. Brushes are a consumable and brushless motors outlast them substantially.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical HP Gains</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-50%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">More runtime per charge</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Maintained</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Speed under load</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Much longer</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Motor life, no brush wear</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Cooler</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tool and battery under load</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="not-worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where It Is Not Worth Paying For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Being honest about this is more useful than a blanket recommendation, because the premium is real money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Occasional-use drills and drivers. If your drill hangs a few pictures, assembles the occasional flat pack and drills the odd hole in a stud, a standard brushed ONE+ drill does that perfectly well and will do it for many years. The brushes will not wear out in domestic use, and the runtime difference is irrelevant when the tool runs for ninety seconds at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low-draw accessories. Work lights, inflators, glue guns, small vacuums, radios, fans. These barely load the battery at all, so neither half of HP does anything. Buy the standard versions and put your old tired packs on them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sanders and rotary tools. These are moderate, steady loads rather than demanding ones, and the practical difference is small. HP versions are nicer but this is where the money returns least.
          </p>

          <p>
            Any tool you are buying to try something once. Cordless platforms tempt people into buying a tool for a single project. If that is what is happening, buy the cheaper version, and upgrade later if the tool turns out to earn its place. The compatibility promise means nothing is stranded either way.
          </p>

          <p>
            And be wary of upgrading a tool you already own and are happy with. The gains are real but they are gains, not fixes. If your existing brushed drill does what you need, the money is better spent on an HP tool in a category you do not yet have covered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Price Premium and Whether It Pays Back
          </h2>

          <p>
            The premium varies by tool but the pattern is consistent enough to plan around.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Standard, Typical</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">HP, Typical</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Premium</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drill / driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50-70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$80-110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~50%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Impact driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$60-80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$90-130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~55%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Circular saw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$70-100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$120-170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~65%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Reciprocating saw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$70-90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$120-160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~70%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">String trimmer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$90-120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$150-200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~65%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery 4.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$70-90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$100-130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~45%</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are indicative rather than current prices, and Ryobi discounts heavily and frequently, so the real-world gap at any given moment is often much smaller. The single most effective buying tactic on this platform is patience: HP tools appear in bundle deals regularly, and the effective premium during a promotion can be close to zero.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether it pays back depends entirely on use frequency. For a tradesperson, the runtime gain alone justifies it within weeks, because fewer battery swaps is directly less time. For someone using a tool monthly, there is no financial payback at all, and the decision is about whether the better experience is worth the money to you. That is a legitimate reason to buy something and it is worth being honest that it is the actual reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            The battery premium is the one most worth paying regardless. HP packs work in everything you own now and everything you might buy later, they run cooler which extends their own life, and they are the half of the equation you cannot easily retrofit when you eventually buy a demanding tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing between an HP tool with a standard battery and a standard tool with an HP battery at the same price, take the second for a demanding job and the first for a light one. The pack outlives the tool and serves the whole platform.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decision" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Decision Table
          </h2>

          <p>
            Find your situation rather than applying a blanket rule.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Battery</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Occasional DIY drilling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard 2.0 Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Regular DIY, some cutting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP for saws, standard for drill</td>
