                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 4.0-6.0 Ah, several</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cordless outdoor equipment</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP, no exceptions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 6.0-12.0 Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lights, inflators, accessories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Old packs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Buying for one project</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whatever you have</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pattern in that table is that HP earns its money on tools that sustain high current — saws, outdoor equipment, impact wrenches — and returns little on tools that draw in short light bursts. Splitting your buying along that line gets most of the benefit for a fraction of the total premium.
          </p>

          <p>
            On batteries the answer is simpler: buy HP from now on. They work in everything, they are what a future demanding tool will need, and they run cooler in the tools you already own. Keep the standard packs you have rather than replacing them, and demote the tired ones to the work light.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last consideration. If you are choosing a platform rather than a tool, and your work genuinely justifies brushless throughout, it is worth comparing against the professional brands before committing, since the price gap narrows once you are buying HP. Our comparison of Milwaukee and Ryobi covers that question, and our cordless tool brand guide ranks the platforms.
          </p>

          <p>
            But for the very large number of people who own a Ryobi drill, some batteries, and an accumulating collection of green tools, the answer is usually this: standard for the light stuff, HP for anything that cuts, HP batteries from here on, and wait for a bundle deal.
          </p>
