        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an HP Tool and Running It on a Small Standard Pack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                HP is a brushless motor plus a high-output battery, and the tool is limited by whichever half is weaker. A 1.5 or 2.0 amp-hour standard pack cannot supply what a brushless saw can accept, so you have paid for performance you cannot reach and the tool will feel disappointing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming HP Means Higher Voltage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every ONE+ battery is 18 volts, including every HP pack. HP describes higher-output cells and supporting electronics, not volts. Anyone telling you an HP pack is a voltage upgrade has misread the naming, and there is no voltage difference to exploit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying the Premium on Low-Draw Tools</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Work lights, inflators, glue guns, small vacuums and occasional-use drills barely load a battery, so neither the brushless motor nor the high-output pack does anything measurable. Buy the standard versions of these and spend the difference on an HP saw.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Upgrading a Brushed Tool You Are Already Happy With</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The gains are genuine improvements rather than fixes for a deficiency. If your existing brushed drill does what you need, replacing it returns less than buying an HP tool in a category you do not yet own. Platform compatibility means nothing is stranded by waiting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying Full Price on This Platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi discounts heavily and runs frequent bundle promotions, often including a free tool with a battery kit. The effective HP premium during a promotion can be close to zero. Patience is the single most effective buying tactic in this range, and the tools do not change often enough to make waiting costly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does HP mean on Ryobi tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High Performance. On a tool it means the motor is brushless, using electronic commutation instead of carbon brushes, which is more efficient, runs cooler and holds speed under load. On a battery it means higher-output cells able to deliver more continuous current. The voltage is unchanged at 18 volts either way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi ONE+ brushless?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some of it. The ONE+ HP range is brushless; the standard ONE+ range is brushed. Both are 18 volts and both use the same batteries, so they coexist on the platform. Check for the HP badge on the tool, because the two sit side by side on the same shelf in similar packaging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need HP batteries for HP tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                To get the performance you paid for, yes. A brushless HP tool can accept far more current than a brushed one, but only if the pack can supply it. On a small standard battery the tool is limited by the battery rather than the motor, which is the commonest reason people conclude HP was oversold.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi HP worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For anything that cuts — circular saws, reciprocating saws, chainsaws — and for cordless outdoor equipment, yes, clearly. For occasional-use drills and drivers, work lights and inflators, no. The gains come from sustained high-current demand, which light intermittent tools never generate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an HP battery work in an older Ryobi tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, perfectly, and it often runs cooler and holds voltage better than the pack originally supplied. Every ONE+ battery fits every ONE+ tool regardless of generation. The only downsides to using an HP pack in a light tool are the extra cost and weight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much longer does an HP tool run per charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 20 to 50 percent longer on the same battery, because a brushless motor wastes less energy as friction and heat, so more of the pack's capacity reaches the work. The gain is largest in sustained demanding use and smallest in short intermittent bursts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy HP tools or better batteries first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Batteries, generally. An HP pack works in everything you already own, runs cooler which extends its own life, and is what any future demanding tool will require. Tools are replaced more often than packs, and a good battery serves the whole platform rather than one machine.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

