        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            HP is two things: a brushless motor in the tool and a high-output pack in your hand. The performance figures assume both, and buying one half is why people come away thinking the upgrade was oversold. An HP saw on a 1.5 amp-hour standard battery is limited by the battery every time.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Split your buying along the demand line. Standard for drills, drivers, lights and inflators, where the brushes will outlast your interest and the runtime gain is irrelevant. HP for anything that cuts and for all cordless outdoor equipment, where a brushless controller holding speed under load is the whole point. And buy HP batteries from now on regardless, because they work in everything and they are the half you cannot retrofit later.
          </p>
